**Diary — 2026-02-14 — Porting the CUDA engine to HIP on gfx906**

Today I moved the core compute engine from CUDA to HIP on an AMD gfx906 device (Vega/MI50 class). The goal was not a toy demo: I wanted the existing kernel pipeline to compile with `hipcc`, run under ROCm, and produce bit-compatible output for the regression suite.

The first pass was mechanical. I replaced CUDA runtime calls with HIP equivalents:

```cpp
// CUDA
cudaMalloc(&d_x, n * sizeof(float));
cudaMemcpyAsync(d_x, h_x, n * sizeof(float), cudaMemcpyHostToDevice, stream);
kernel<<<grid, block, smem, stream>>>(d_x, d_y, n);
cudaMemcpyAsync(h_y, d_y, n * sizeof(float), cudaMemcpyDeviceToHost, stream);
cudaStreamSynchronize(stream);
```

became:

```cpp
// HIP
hipMalloc(&d_x, n * sizeof(float));
hipMemcpyAsync(d_x, h_x, n * sizeof(float), hipMemcpyHostToDevice, stream);
hipLaunchKernelGGL(kernel, grid, block, smem, stream, d_x, d_y, n);
hipMemcpyAsync(h_y, d_y, n * sizeof(float), hipMemcpyDeviceToHost, stream);
hipStreamSynchronize(stream);
```

I kept the CUDA-style kernel launch syntax in a compatibility header for the first build:

```cpp
#define CUDA_KERNEL_LAUNCH(kernel, grid, block, smem, stream, ...) \
    hipLaunchKernelGGL(kernel, grid, block, smem, stream, __VA_ARGS__)
```

That let me compile the old engine with minimal edits, then remove the shim once the call sites were clean.

The first real gfx906-specific issue was warp semantics. CUDA assumes 32-lane warps; gfx906 uses 64-lane wavefronts. The engine had a reduction kernel that used warp shuffles:

```cpp
__device__ float warp_reduce(float v) {
    for (int offset = 16; offset > 0; offset >>= 1)
        v += __shfl_down_sync(0xffffffff, v, offset);
    return v
